            "review_text": "Duncan_Moore\nHo Chi Minh City, null, Vietnam65 contributions\n0\nConveyor belt hot pot\nMay 2014 • Friends\nThis place is not somewhere to go out for a romantic meal, but rather a bustling restaurant with quite basic decor. Imagine you take the conveyor belt sushi concept and apply it to hot pot.\n\nYou have a choice of three soups - mushroom, hot and sour, or spicy - in your own pot sunken into the counter in front of you. You control the temperature and do your own cooking, similar to Japanese shabu shabu and yakiniku restaurants.\n\nRaw ingredients - a range of meats and seafood, vegetables and noodles - come round on little plates on the conveyor belt and you take whatever you feel like eating and throw it into your soup.\n\nWhat we like about this place is that you can eat all kinds of vegetables in addition to your meat. A lot of Vietnamese food seems quite light on veg, but not Kichi Kichi. Maybe nutritionists recommend eating at least five servings of fruit and veg per day, but I get double that in a single meal at this restaurant. The fact that you're not cooking in oil also makes it easy to eat very healthily here.\n\nCooking your own food takes no time at all and minimises the need for staff, which means low prices (200k VND + 10% tax at the most expensive times) and no half-trained waiters walking around annoying you trying to be helpful, like at a lot of places these days.\n\nWe've been to Kichi Kichi twice, once for a late lunch and once for dinner, like it a lot, and will definitely be back. The Trip Advisor page lists one address, but this is actually a chain with around half a dozen branches in HCMC alone.\nRead more\nValue\nFood\nWritten May 26, 2014",
            "rating": 5.0,
